\subsection{Within-continental scale}

Within Europe, we found that spring predictability was associated with increased chilling and forcing sensitivity, especially when spring predictability was measured as variation in growing degree days to last frost. These patterns qualitatively match previous findings from \citet{Zohner:2017aa}, which report stronger reliance on secondary cues (i.e. chilling) in North America compared to Europe based on substantial differences in STV between the continents. %emwMay12 -- do we need to add ", though we did not find that relationship here" OR -- make the change above and sort of skip the Zohner cross-continental results here? 
But we did not find this relationship in North America, despite the continent spanning a much wider range of spring predictability than Europe. North American however had substantially weaker spring predictability (i.e., higher STV ) than Europe, suggesting that the relationship may occur at certain levels of predictability. Further, our results suggest the exact metric used to approximate `spring predictability' may matter, as we only found a relationship when we used a metric that estimated variability in the consistency of thermal warmth before the last frost (variability in the number of growing degree days before the last frost), a metric closely related to spring forcing cues that may be a more realistic proxy for spring predictability than STV, which measures ... %emwMay12 --  I can help more if you get this started. 
