Our finding of the major difference in spring predictability between Europe and North America supports previous work (CITEZohner I think? Others?) that suggests the arrival of spring in Europe is always relatively predictable from a growing degree day perspective. %emwMay12 -- I personally would add something here about the jet stream likely causing this and riff on what the past might of looked like and what the future has in store. Ben Cook could very likely help! You could send him a short email with the main figures and relevant findings and ask him to give you a little text on what may cause these differences in predictability, how stable they have probably been over time (matching to the next paragraph nicely!) and how stable they will be. CC me if you want and I will help explain as I can. 

The large differences between spring predictability in Europe and North America, combined with strong signals of shared evolutionary history, suggest we should be cautious in interpreting weak relationships as evidence of a major force structuring differences in phenological cues across species. While we found marginal support for the SPH considering the climate patterns experienced by species across their ranges, our results were always weak and often depending on the metric of spring predictability: lower spring predictability was associated with increased reliance on chilling (i.e. stronger chilling sensitivity) when using STV as a metric of predictability, and with increased reliance on photoperiod when using Stdv in GDDs to last frost as a metric for North American species, while for European species spring predictability was associated with increased reliance on chilling (i.e. stronger chilling sensitivity) when we used Stdv in GDDs to last frost as a metric. Further, all other aspects of the predictability/cue relationship either showed no relationship (i.e. forcing \& photoperiod effects with STV in Europe \& North America) or observed relationships contrary to the expectations of the SPH (i.e., chilling in Europe with STV and forcing in North America with Stdv. GDDs). We argue that these mixed results should not serve to refute the claims of the SPH---it is clear that spring predictability has a role to play in shaping where and how species successfully leaf out. However, our study indicates a more complicated picture of the factors that shape species' level differences in phenological cues. %emwMay12 -- I have a personal rule to use leafout as a noun and leaf out as a verb... but up you. 

