\subsection{Bridging from continental to local scales}
% Our study indicates that predictability of spring species' experience across their geographic ranges may play a role in shaping phenological cue sensitivities, there must be more to the story. When considering the fact that many species with divergent phenological cue sensitives have highly overlapping ranges (Make Figure S2) and experience similar environmental conditions (i.e., spring predictability, late season freeze risk, etc) across their ranges, it is worthwhile to consider phenological cues in a community context \citep{}.
Our results---and many previous studies \citet{Zohner:2017aa}---show high variability in cues across species with highly overlapping ranges (Make Figure S2) suggesting continental or even regional variation in climate is unlikely to explain a large portion of the observed variability, while increasing research suggests community-level processes may drive this variation. Numerous studies of species in one location---for example forest trees in one community---have found variability in cues across species (CITElaube,Flynn) 
 of a similar scale to that seen across large scale studies (CITEZOHNER,Ettinger). Ecological theory predicts high variability in many systems where the conditions in the early season (e.g., very early spring in many temperate forests) is both more risky due to spring frost---as we have focused on here---but also rich in nutrient and light resources. Species may thus assemble each season along a gradient of growth strategies: leafout early and experience a resource-rich and low-competition, but riskier, environment, or leafout later and experience a lower resource, higher competition but less risky environment \citep{Sakai1987,Rathcke1985,Lopez2008}. Such a trade-off makes even more sense from a functional trait framework, which predicts early leafing species to posses traits indicative of less costly leaf and wood tissues that are easier to replace \citep{Reich1997,Wright2004}, while also being less competitive under low resource environments (and vice versa). Indeed, increasing research is finding support for such relationships between leaf out and plant traits \citep{Wang2021,Sporbert2022}, suggesting growth strategies and leafout timing within a community may be most predictive of variation in cues across species. 
 
The importance of leafout timing within a community context further suggests we would only expect spring frost predictability to be important for a subset of species that are generally leafing out early across their communities. Thus understanding how spring frost predictability affects phenological cues may be best approached through new studies that consider both better metrics of spring predictability and community context that integrates functional traits. Research has long shown that different species have evolved multiple ways to handle spring frosts: late-leafout species effectively avoid it altogether, while early-leafout species may tolerate it through multiple pathways---building tissues that can withstand lower temperatures (through wax, trichomes etc.) or through the ability to quickly rebuild leaves. Diversity in species growth strategies and traits can lead to differences in species temporal niche, alleviating competition within a community and promoting greater species coexistence \citep{Fargione2005}. These realities, alongside our findings and increasing research on woody plant phenology within communities and functional trait frameworks, suggest better predictions may come from new studies that tackle this variation, while also controlling for phylogenetic relatedness, which has been repeatedly shown to predict species' freeze tolerance and related traits (CITES, Zanne -- see who cited Zanne for some recent stuff? And Nacho should have some refs!).
 
At the same time, future work should include sub-species-level variation to better understand how the populations that underlie species-level variation will respond to a warming climate. Our results add to the debate about the extent to which local adaptation shapes phenological cues \citep{Zeng2024}, and it is likely the strength of local adaptation variation among species, and phenological phases themselves \citep{Zeng2024,Savolainen2007}. We find that species-level variation clearly swamps population-level variation in cues, but that different studies also vary substantially even after accounting for most treatment, species and population-level effects---though we found data to test this was highly limited. This suggests fully teasing out the relative effects will require more data alongside models that can fully partition variation. Given that ancestral alleles may shape current climatic sub-populations, more work on populations and sampling across species may be most useful for predicting plant responses to climate change. % maybe cite https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adp5717

